<TopicPage slug="subjects/materials-science/advanced/phase-diagrams">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Phase diagrams are <strong>thermodynamic maps of alloys</strong> — what exists, in what
    proportion, at each temperature and composition. Metallurgy became a science when these
    maps were drawn: blacksmith wisdom became itinerary planning on the iron–carbon diagram.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the map, the lever, the eutectic, iron–carbon" widget="equationStep" config={phaseCfg} />

  <h2>What the maps unlock</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Steel\'s phase dance:</strong> heat into austenite (carbon dissolved), then cool at chosen speeds — fast cooling traps carbon (martensite: hard, brittle — quenched steel), slow cooling lets it segregate (pearlite: soft, tough). The entire heat-treatment lesson is travel on this one diagram.</li>
    <li><strong>Why stainless is stainless:</strong> chromium above ~12% forms a self-healing oxide film — a phase-map boundary that also happens to defeat rust; nickel stabilises the tough austenite structure at room temperature (the 18/8 recipe of cutlery).</li>
    <li><strong>Superalloys: diagrams with design goals:</strong> nickel alloys engineered so the strengthening phase (gamma-prime) dissolves just above engine operating temperature — the diagram IS the jet-engine performance envelope (the single-crystal lesson\'s context).</li>
    <li><strong>Computational phase diagrams (CALPHAD):</strong> thermodynamic databases now predict multi-component maps (6+ elements) before any experiment — the expert lesson\'s frontier, and how new alloys are screened in weeks instead of years.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Callister; the International Iron & Steel Institute phase-diagram references; CALPHAD methodology (Saunders & Miodownik).</p>
</TopicPage>
